#
# EXP-2: the trail does not flicker.
#
# This seam repaints at the *return* of the program's own back-buffer
# present, which is reached by every path that puts a screen up — the
# window's redraw and each step of the party icon's animation. The design
# decision behind that is in `seam_explored.cpp`, and the risk it names
# is the one only a run can answer: a repaint that lands a moment after
# the program's own would show as the trail blinking off and on.
#
# **A single leg, because a pair cannot say it.** A pair compares two
# runs; this is a claim about one run over time. It is `same`: with the
# party standing still and nothing pressed, fifty-one consecutive stills
# of the screen are the same screen. A repaint that arrived late, or a
# present the seam did not paint after, would move one of them.
#
# Measured before it was written down: over the walk in `exp-trail.leg`
# the seam's points are reached 746,746 times, and from the frame the
# arrival settles at to the end of the run every dumped still has the fog
# on it — none of the 112 that differ from the seam-off run is missing
# it, and the 51 in the range below are one still repeated.
about the fog holds still while the party does
kind single
program START.EXE
both code-wheel
# The code-wheel bypass is gated on the wheel itself (#115). Pinned by
# digest because a document never enters this tree.
document 0db301aeac4d2ec1e63b409ca6d3c9d39c63381d6712ff2ce7edc0528c6586fd
seam explored
until 260000000
every 25

key A@7601
key Return@7651
key L@8951
key J@9201
key Up@10600
key Up@10750
key Up@10900
key Up@11050
key Up@11200
key Up@11350
key Up@11500
key Up@11650

# From the frame after the last step to the end of the run: the party is
# standing on the wilderness map inside the corridor it uncovered, with
# the fog round it, and nothing about the screen may change.
same 11750-13000
